## Part 1: Introduction to reactivity

This week you'll venture further into what you can do with Svelte by learning how to create reactive components.

[Reactivity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reactive_programming) is the arcane term that developers use to describe features that respond instantly to new data or user input. It's what allows news developers to create engaging applications that wouldn't be possible in print or a one-way broadcast.

Interactive features can take many different forms. They can be quizzes, calculators, polls, games, maps, dashboards, rankings and anything else that allows the page to automatically update in response to user actions.

A famous example is ["How Y’all, Youse and You Guys Talk,"](https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2014/upshot/dialect-quiz-map.html) a dialect quiz published in 2013 by [Josh Katz](https://www.nytimes.com/by/josh-katz) and [Wilson Andrews](https://www.nytimes.com/by/wilson-andrews) of The New York Times.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-4/nyt-dialect-quiz.png"
  alt="The New York Times dialect quiz showing a heat map of the United States based on reader answers about regional vocabulary"
  chromeTitle="How Y'all, Youse and You Guys Talk"
  chromeUrl="https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2014/upshot/dialect-quiz-map.html"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

It asks readers a series of questions about the words they choose — do you say "sneakers" or "tennis shoes"? — and then generates a personalized heat map that pinpoints where they are likely to have grown up. It rocketed across the web, ranking as the The Times' [most popular page of the year](https://www.nytco.com/press/the-new-york-timess-most-visited-content-of-2013/), and [earning Katz](https://knightlab.northwestern.edu/2014/01/20/behind-the-dialect-map-interactive-how-an-intern-created-the-new-york-times-most-popular-piece-of-content-in-2013/), who authored it as an intern, a full-time job.

<Blockquote
  quote="I’m pretty blown away by the response to the whole thing."
  attribution="Josh Katz"
/>

Today you're going to learn the fundamentals you need to build interactive components like the Times quiz. Each part will introduce a concept you need to know.

